Consider 10-dimensional type II supergravity (type IIA or type IIB) with localized sources consisting of orientifold Op-planes and/or Dp-branes of a single, common dimension p (so that all sources are mutually parallel, i.e., they all wrap the same internal directions). Adopt the standard smeared ansatz in which:

(i) the 10-dimensional spacetime is a warped product M_4 × M_6, where M_4 is 4-dimensional de Sitter space (with strictly positive cosmological constant) and M_6 is a compact 6-dimensional group manifold (the quotient of a 6-dimensional Lie group by a discrete cocompact subgroup);
(ii) the dilaton is constant, the warp factor is trivial, and the contributions of the Op/Dp sources are smeared (uniformly distributed) over the directions transverse to them inside M_6;
(iii) all NSNS and RR fluxes are taken to be constant in the left-invariant basis of M_6;
(iv) the equations of motion of the bosonic sector of type II supergravity (10-dimensional Einstein equations, dilaton equation, flux Bianchi identities and equations of motion, and source contributions consistent with the appropriate tadpole conditions) are satisfied.

Question: Does there exist any choice of p ∈ {0, 1, …, 8}, of the 6-dimensional group manifold M_6, and of constant fluxes such that the above ansatz yields a bona fide solution with M_4 = dS_4? Equivalently, prove or disprove the conjecture that no classical de Sitter solution of 10-dimensional type II supergravity exists in the parallel-sources, smeared, group-manifold, constant-flux setting.

